## At a glance

| Dimension | Langfuse | Tracify |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Model** | Open-source (self-hosted) + commercial cloud | SaaS-only, commercial |
| **Core strength** | Flexible, self-hosted LLM tracing; owns data | Integrated tracing, evaluation, cost tracking; fast iteration |
| **Self-hosting** | Full (Docker, Postgres, Redis, operators) | Not available |
| **Evaluation framework** | Limited; manual + API-based tests | Integrated; native test library, batch runner, LLM-as-judge |
| **Cost tracking** | Token counting at span level; manual pricing integration | Automatic real-time per-call cost breakdown by model/provider |
| **Session tracking** | Trace metadata; build session correlation yourself | Native multi-turn sessions with context preservation |
| **Production readiness** | Strong for tracing; evaluation is add-on | Complete out-of-box (tracing + evaluation + cost + sessions) |
| **Setup time** | Self-hosted: 2-4 weeks; cloud: 1 week | 15 minutes (API key + instrumentation) |
| **Team size required** | 2+ FTE for self-hosted infrastructure | 0.1 FTE for operations |
| **Monthly cost (100k calls)** | Self-hosted: ~$7,700; Cloud: ~$3,400 | ~$2,200 (all-in with evaluation) |
| **Best for** | Teams needing data residency, architectural control | Teams prioritizing speed, evaluation velocity, cost transparency |

### The operating model

**Langfuse** emphasizes *tracing transparency and operational control*: you host traces in your infrastructure (or use their managed cloud), own the data flow, and compose evaluation and experimentation tools as you need them. **Tracify** emphasizes *integrated evaluation and fast production iteration*: comprehensive tracing + native evaluation framework + automatic cost tracking + session management as one product. Choose Langfuse when data residency, infrastructure control, or architectural customization are requirements. Choose Tracify when evaluation velocity, cost visibility, and time-to-first-insight matter more than self-hosting.

## Opening situation: the three-month production deadline

Your team is shipping a customer-facing multi-agent system in three months. The agents handle customer support routing, information retrieval, and action execution across Claude, GPT-4, and Mixtral models. Your engineering leads, product manager, and infrastructure team need answers to these questions:

**For engineering leads:** How do we detect quality regressions before they hit production? How much overhead is evaluation infrastructure?

**For infrastructure teams:** Do we self-host tracing to keep data in-house, or use a managed service? What's the operational tax?

**For product managers:** What's our true cost per request? Can we optimize model selection and routing based on per-call costs and quality metrics?

**For founders/operators:** What's the real 12-month total cost of ownership? How much engineering time do we need to allocate to platform operations vs. product iteration?

This article walks through the real operating differences, shows concrete scenarios where each platform shines, and names the trade-offs you are making with each decision.

## Core difference: flexibility vs. completeness

{% highlight %}The choice between Langfuse and Tracify is not primarily about cost or feature parity. It is about whether your team's pace of reaching production confidence is bounded by infrastructure setup and operational overhead, or by the platform's evaluation and cost-tracking capabilities.{% /highlight %}

**Langfuse** is built for *teams that own their infrastructure and want composable pieces*. You get excellent, battle-tested trace collection with rich SDKs, the ability to self-host on your infrastructure, and control over every part of the data flow. You integrate evaluation, cost optimization, and experimentation using external tools, custom code, or vendor integrations. The upside: total architectural control, data residency, and the ability to optimize the stack for your specific needs. The downside: you own setup, scaling, maintenance, and glue code.

**Tracify** is built for *teams that want integrated evaluation and cost visibility from day one*. Tracing, evaluation, cost tracking, experiments, and session management are unified in one product. You give up self-hosting but gain feature velocity and move from "we have traces" to "we have actionable quality gates and cost optimization" in days instead of weeks. The upside: fast iteration, automatic cost transparency, and built-in evaluation framework. The downside: SaaS-only model, and you move at the platform's feature velocity, not your own.

## The architecture comparison

### Langfuse: modular, self-hosted control

Langfuse's strength is architectural clarity and operational control. You have two paths:

**Path 1: Self-hosted (maximum control)**
- Deploy the entire platform on your infrastructure: Docker containers, Postgres database, Redis cache, ingestion servers.
- Preserve all trace data in your own VPC (critical for regulated industries and data residency requirements).
- Audit the source code, make security-relevant changes, patch zero-days immediately.
- Integrate custom evaluation tooling using Langfuse's trace API: wire your LLM-as-judge evaluators, connect external ML services, build custom test harnesses.
- Use the commercial cloud for cost optimization (more on pricing below) or cross-region failover.

**Path 2: Langfuse cloud (managed, but not integrated evaluation)**
- Langfuse runs the infrastructure; you focus on instrumentation and integration.
- Data still lives in Langfuse's infrastructure (similar compliance model to other SaaS).
- Pay per event, similar to Tracify, but with less built-in evaluation infrastructure.

The trade-off: architectural flexibility in exchange for operational overhead. If you choose self-hosting, you own 0-2 FTE of engineering time for setup, scaling, and maintenance. If you choose cloud, you own evaluation integration work.

### Tracify: integrated, SaaS-only

Tracify's strength is production completeness out-of-the-box. You get:

- **No infrastructure.** Grab an API key, add the SDK to your code, and start sending traces within 15 minutes. Everything else—ingestion, storage, scaling, backups—is managed.
- **Integrated evaluation.** Define test cases in the dashboard, create evaluation conditions (LLM-as-judge, exact match, semantic similarity), and run batch evaluations against your traces. Version your test cases and track regression metrics over time.
- **Automatic cost tracking.** Real-time per-call cost breakdown by model, provider, and routing decision. Tracify fetches pricing from OpenAI, Anthropic, and other providers; you don't manually integrate pricing data.
- **Native session management.** Multi-turn agent interactions are first-class. Cost and quality metrics roll up at the session level, not just individual calls.
- **Experiment framework.** Compare prompt versions, model selections, or routing strategies with statistical significance testing built-in.

The trade-off: SaaS-only means no self-hosting, no data residency in your VPC, and features evolve at Tracify's cadence. This is a boundary condition for regulated industries.

## Scenario 1: Multi-agent production system with cost optimization

Your team is shipping a customer-facing agent that routes support requests to Claude, GPT-4, or Mixtral depending on complexity, then takes actions based on the response. You need to:
- Understand per-request costs by model selection
- Detect quality regressions in LLM outputs
- Iterate on routing rules based on cost and quality trade-offs
- Hit production within eight weeks

### With Langfuse (self-hosted)

**Timeline: Setup phase (2-3 weeks)**
1. Provision infrastructure: Postgres (primary + read replica), Redis, ingestion servers on Kubernetes. ($1,200/month + 1-2 FTE for setup and scaling decisions)
2. Instrument your agent code with Langfuse SDK:

```typescript
// Illustrative: span-level instrumentation
import { Langfuse } from "langfuse";

const langfuse = new Langfuse();

async function routeAndExecute(userMessage: string) {
  const trace = langfuse.trace({ name: "support-agent-run" });

  const routingSpan = trace.span({ name: "routing-decision" });
  const complexity = await analyzeComplexity(userMessage);
  const selectedModel = complexity > 0.7 ? "gpt-4" : "claude-3";
  routingSpan.end({ output: selectedModel });

  const llmSpan = trace.span({ name: "llm-call", input: { model: selectedModel } });
  const response = await callLLM(selectedModel, userMessage);
  // Token counts: you must manually track this or integrate usage data
  llmSpan.end({ output: response, metadata: { tokens: response.usage } });
}
```

3. Sync traces to Langfuse (you choose: real-time or batched ingestion).

**Timeline: Evaluation phase (3-4 weeks)**
- Traces are flowing, but now you need evaluation. Build an evaluation runner that:
  - Pulls traces from Langfuse API
  - Runs your quality checks (LLM-as-judge, regex pattern matching, semantic similarity)
  - Stores results somewhere (Postgres, Langfuse metadata)
  - Notifies you of regressions
- Integrate provider pricing (OpenAI, Anthropic, Mistral pricing data into your analysis) to calculate true per-request cost.
- Build dashboards or reports to visualize cost by model selection and quality metrics.

**Result:** By week 5-6, you have traces and basic cost reporting. Evaluation infrastructure is still in development. Your first quality gate is ready by week 7-8 if you have dedicated engineering bandwidth.

### With Tracify

**Timeline: Setup phase (15-30 minutes)**
1. Create a Tracify account, grab your API key.
2. Instrument your code (same as Langfuse, slightly different SDK):

```typescript
// Illustrative: Tracify instrumentation (similar structure)
import { Tracify } from "@tracify/sdk";

const tracify = new Tracify({ apiKey: process.env.TRACIFY_API_KEY });

async function routeAndExecute(userMessage: string) {
  const session = tracify.session({ userId: user.id, sessionId: sessionId });

  const routingSpan = session.span({ name: "routing-decision" });
  const complexity = await analyzeComplexity(userMessage);
  const selectedModel = complexity > 0.7 ? "gpt-4" : "claude-3";
  routingSpan.end({ output: selectedModel });

  const llmSpan = session.span({ name: "llm-call", input: { model: selectedModel } });
  const response = await callLLM(selectedModel, userMessage);
  llmSpan.end({ output: response }); // Token counts automatic
}
```

3. Traces are live immediately. Cost breakdown is automatic (Tracify fetches real pricing).

**Timeline: Evaluation phase (Day 1-2)**
- Open the Tracify dashboard.
- Create test cases: "support question should route to GPT-4 if complexity > 0.7" and "response should not contain profanity."
- Batch-run evaluations against your existing traces.
- See cost breakdown by model, routing decision, and session (all automatic).
- Set up cost alerts: "if average cost per session exceeds $0.50, notify team."

**Result:** By Day 2-3, you have traces, cost visibility, and running quality gates. You iterate on routing rules and prompt versions using the experiment framework. No evaluation infrastructure to build.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Production velocity" title="Integrated evaluation changes the pace of feedback." body="With Langfuse, you have excellent traces but still must architect and build evaluation infrastructure. With Tracify, evaluation is part of the product from day one. This difference compounds: by week 8, Langfuse teams are still building evaluation plumbing; Tracify teams are optimizing routing based on real metrics. {% highlight %}The platform you choose does not determine your tracing quality—both are excellent. It determines how quickly you move from 'we can see what happened' to 'we can optimize what happens next.'{% /highlight %}" tone="yellow" /%}

## Scenario 2: Regulated industry with strict data residency

Your team operates in financial services or healthcare and must keep all production data in your VPC. Compliance audits, data residency requirements, and infrastructure control are non-negotiable.

### With Langfuse (self-hosted only)

- **Self-host the entire platform** in your Kubernetes cluster or on-premise infrastructure. Postgres database, Redis cache, and ingestion servers all run in your VPC behind your firewalls.
- **Audit the source code.** Langfuse is open-source; your security team can review it, make modifications, and patch zero-days immediately.
- **Traces never leave your network.** All LLM call data, prompt inputs, and outputs stay in your infrastructure. No data escapes to a third-party cloud.
- **Build evaluation and analysis tools** within your existing stack. Wire your evaluation to your data warehouse, ML platform, or custom analysis tools.
- **Retain long-term data.** You decide retention policies, archival strategy, and who can access data.

**Operational cost:** 0.5-1.5 FTE for setup, scaling, and ongoing maintenance. Self-hosted infrastructure ($1,200-2,000/month depending on scale).

**Result:** Full control. You own every operational decision but inherit the burden of running and maintaining the platform.

### With Tracify

- **SaaS-only model.** No self-hosting option available.
- **Data residency requirement is a disqualifying constraint.** Traces are stored in Tracify's infrastructure, not yours.
- **This scenario is a boundary condition where Tracify is not an option.**

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="When self-hosting is required" title="Langfuse is the only viable choice for data residency constraints." body="If your regulatory posture demands that all LLM call data stays in your VPC, Tracify's SaaS-only model is disqualifying. Langfuse's self-hosted option solves this constraint completely. You give up the convenience of managed infrastructure, but you gain data control and architectural flexibility. Estimate 0.5-1.5 FTE for operations and $1,200-2,000/month for infrastructure." tone="dark" /%}

> **Compliance note:** Some regulated organizations use Langfuse cloud (Langfuse's managed SaaS) instead of self-hosting, accepting data residency in Langfuse's infrastructure provided Langfuse signs a Data Processing Agreement (DPA). This is a middle ground if you want managed infrastructure but need contractual data protection. Verify Langfuse's compliance posture with your legal and security teams before committing.

## Deep comparison: feature by feature

### Tracing and instrumentation

**Langfuse:**
- Excellent SDK support: Python, Node.js, Rust, with active community contributions.
- Decorator-based instrumentation (`@langfuse()` decorator) and manual span creation via client API.
- Span-level metadata: model name, input/output tokens, custom tags, user IDs, session IDs.
- Deep integrations: LangChain, LlamaIndex, Vercel AI SDK, and manual instrumentation for custom frameworks.
- SDKs are stable and battle-tested in production at hundreds of companies.

**Tracify:**
- Equivalent SDK support: Python, Node.js, TypeScript/JavaScript, with TypeScript-first design.
- Instrumentation: decorator-based (`@tracify()`) and imperative client API.
- Span-level metadata plus automatic session tracking (multi-turn conversations are tracked as a unit).
- Integrations: LangChain, LlamaIndex, native instrumentation for OpenAI, Anthropic, and Mistral APIs.
- SDKs designed specifically for agent workflows, not generic LLM tracing.

**Winner:** Tie. Both provide excellent tracing instrumentation. Langfuse has longer history and broader community integrations. Tracify is narrowly optimized for agent patterns, which is an advantage if your use case is agent-centric.

### Evaluation framework

**Langfuse:**
- **Manual evaluation through the dashboard:** Mark traces as good/bad, add scores and comments.
- **API-based evaluation:** Call the Langfuse API from external scripts to run automated evaluations, store results.
- **No test case management:** You maintain evaluation criteria outside the platform (markdown, spreadsheets, external test runners).
- **No experiment framework:** Comparing two prompt versions requires manual trace analysis or external tools.
- **Extensibility:** You control the evaluation logic entirely; integration with custom ML models or scoring functions is possible but requires engineering.

**Tracify:**
- **Native test case library:** Define test cases in the dashboard with parameters and expected outputs.
- **Batch evaluation runner:** Run evaluations against historical traces with a single click.
- **Parameterized evaluations:** Reuse test cases across experiments, version them, and track changes.
- **LLM-as-judge evaluators:** Built-in semantic evaluators (exact match, substring, LLM-judged similarity) require no external tools.
- **Experiment framework:** Compare prompt versions, model selections, or routing strategies with statistical significance testing.
- **Regression detection:** Automated alerts when evaluation metrics drop below a threshold.

**Winner:** Tracify. {% highlight %}Evaluation is where the separation becomes decisive. Langfuse evaluation requires external tooling and orchestration; Tracify's native evaluation framework is production-tested and designed for iterative agent development.{% /highlight %} If you are planning evaluation as an afterthought, Langfuse is adequate. If you want evaluation integrated into your development workflow, Tracify is a force multiplier.

### Cost tracking and analysis

**Langfuse:**
- **Token counting:** Langfuse records input and output tokens at the span level (model API provides these).
- **No built-in pricing:** Langfuse does not know the cost of a token. You must integrate OpenAI, Anthropic, or other pricing into your analysis.
- **Cost breakdown requires manual work:** Export traces, calculate costs in your data warehouse or scripts, build dashboards externally.
- **Result:** You have the data (tokens) but not the insight (cost per request, cost by model, cost trending).

**Tracify:**
- **Automatic cost calculation:** Tracify fetches real-time pricing from OpenAI, Anthropic, Mistral, and other providers. Every span is automatically costed.
- **Per-call breakdown:** Each LLM call shows exact cost, input tokens, output tokens, and which model was used.
- **Cost trending:** Charts showing cost per session, cost per agent, cost trending over time.
- **Cost recommendations:** Alerts when cost per request exceeds targets, suggestions to optimize model selection or batch processing.
- **Session-level rollup:** Multi-turn conversations show total cost and cost distribution across turns.

**Winner:** Tracify. {% highlight %}Cost transparency is a strategic advantage if you operate multiple models. Tracify's automatic pricing means cost optimization is accessible to product teams, not just engineers with data warehouse access.{% /highlight %}

### Session and context tracking

**Langfuse:**
- **Trace-level metadata:** Sessions, user IDs, tags, custom fields are available as span metadata.
- **No native session concept:** You build session correlation into instrumentation by explicitly setting session IDs on every span.
- **Result:** Sessions are possible but require discipline in instrumentation. Context across multiple agent steps is retrievable but not primary.

**Tracify:**
- **Native session tracking:** Multi-turn agent interactions are tracked as one session unit automatically.
- **Context preservation:** Agent state, previous turns, and session-level decisions are captured and queryable.
- **Session-level metrics:** Quality and cost metrics roll up at the session level, not just individual LLM calls.
- **Result:** Multi-turn workflows are first-class citizens, not an instrumentation detail.

**Winner:** Tracify. For agent systems where a user interaction spans multiple LLM calls, session-level tracking is not a nice-to-have; it is essential.

### Hosted infrastructure and operations

**Langfuse:**
- **Self-hosted:** You operate the entire platform. Requires Kubernetes or equivalent infrastructure, database management, and on-call coverage.
- **Langfuse cloud:** Managed SaaS option where Langfuse runs the infrastructure. Less operational overhead but data residency in their cloud.
- **Scaling decisions:** You (self-hosted) or Langfuse (cloud) decide scaling strategy.

**Tracify:**
- **SaaS only:** Tracify manages all infrastructure, scaling, backups, and uptime.
- **No self-hosting:** Data residency in Tracify's infrastructure is non-negotiable.
- **Operations cost:** Minimal; focus is on product features, not platform maintenance.

**Winner:** Depends on your constraint. **Self-hosting required?** Langfuse (only option). **Operational simplicity?** Tracify (no ops at all). **Data residency concerns?** Langfuse cloud with DPA, or self-hosted Langfuse.

## Cost analysis: Total cost of ownership over 12 months

Assume a team shipping a production agent system with 100k LLM calls per month, 10 engineers, and a three-month time-to-production deadline.

### Langfuse self-hosted

| Category | Item | Cost |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Infrastructure** | 2x Postgres (primary + replica), Redis, ingestion servers on Kubernetes | $1,200/month |
| **Setup (one-time)** | Design, provisioning, security hardening, monitoring (2 weeks) | $2,400 |
| **Operations** | On-call coverage, scaling, Postgres maintenance, patching (0.5 FTE) | $6,000/month |
| **Evaluation infrastructure** | Custom evaluation runner, external ML service integration, dashboards | $500/month + 1 FTE setup |
| **Lost productivity** | Time your engineers spend on platform operations instead of product | Embedded in 0.5 FTE |
| **Total monthly** | | **$7,700** |
| **Total annual** | | **$92,400** |
| **Time-to-production** | Tracing: week 3; Evaluation: week 8 | ~8 weeks |

### Langfuse commercial cloud (managed)

| Category | Item | Cost |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Platform** | Cloud hosting, ingestion, storage (100k events/month pricing) | $500/month |
| **Setup (one-time)** | Integration, verification, team onboarding (1 week) | $1,200 |
| **Operations** | Monitoring, troubleshooting, vendor support | Minimal; ~0.1 FTE |
| **Evaluation infrastructure** | External LLM-as-judge service, test runners, dashboards | $500/month + 1 FTE setup |
| **Glue code maintenance** | Keeping evaluation bridge code operational (0.2 FTE) | $2,400/month |
| **Total monthly** | | **$3,400** |
| **Total annual** | | **$40,800** |
| **Time-to-production** | Tracing: week 1; Evaluation: week 6 | ~6 weeks |

### Tracify (integrated SaaS)

| Category | Item | Cost |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Platform** | Cloud hosting, tracing, evaluation, cost tracking, experiments (100k events/month) | $1,000/month |
| **Setup (one-time)** | Account creation, SDK integration, first test case (2 days) | $400 |
| **Operations** | None; managed end-to-end by Tracify | $0/month |
| **Evaluation** | Built-in test case library, LLM-as-judge, batch runner | Included |
| **Maintenance** | Minor: updating SDK, experimenting with features (0.1 FTE, mostly product-facing) | $1,200/month |
| **Total monthly** | | **$2,200** |
| **Total annual** | | **$26,400** |
| **Time-to-production** | Tracing + evaluation + cost tracking: day 2 | ~2-3 days |

{% highlight %}The self-hosted model trades platform costs (~$1,200/month) for engineering time (0.5-1.5 FTE at $6,000-18,000/month). Tracify's all-in-one model is 3-4x cheaper on an annual basis and reaches production **5-6 weeks faster** because evaluation infrastructure is not a separate phase.{% /highlight %}

> **For small teams (< 5 engineers):** Langfuse self-hosted is typically not worth the operational overhead. Langfuse cloud or Tracify are more realistic choices.
>
> **For large teams (> 20 engineers):** Langfuse self-hosted becomes more viable if you have dedicated infrastructure and ML engineering teams. The operational cost ($6k-12k/month) is absorbed more easily in larger orgs.
>
> **For regulated teams:** Langfuse self-hosted is often the only option that satisfies compliance requirements, regardless of company size. The operational overhead is a fixed requirement, not a choice.

## Trade-offs and honest boundaries

### Choose Langfuse when:

**1. Data residency is non-negotiable (regulated industries).**
- Your compliance requirements mandate that all LLM call data stays in your VPC.
- You cannot use SaaS platforms that store data outside your infrastructure.
- **Mitigation:** Langfuse self-hosting is the only option; budget 0.5-1.5 FTE for operations.

**2. You have infrastructure expertise and love operational ownership.**
- Your team operates Kubernetes, manages Postgres at scale, and treats infrastructure as a competitive advantage.
- You want to patch zero-days immediately, audit security patches, and make performance-critical changes.
- **Mitigation:** Self-hosting gives you maximum control. Plan 2-3 weeks for setup, then 0.5-1 FTE ongoing.

**3. Your evaluation needs are highly specialized.**
- You are building custom ML evaluation pipelines, integrating with proprietary scoring functions, or using external ML services.
- You want full control over which evaluation tool connects to your tracing.
- **Mitigation:** Langfuse's trace API is excellent; external evaluation tools integrate cleanly. You own the glue code.

**4. You have existing Langfuse instrumentation.**
- Migration costs (rewriting spans, redirecting SDKs) are sunk. Staying with Langfuse is the path of least resistance.
- **Mitigation:** Estimate migration effort (usually 2-4 weeks for large codebases); if you are staying >12 months, migration may be worth it.

### Choose Tracify when:

**1. Production confidence is your primary constraint.**
- You have three months to ship. Evaluation infrastructure cannot be a separate phase; it must be integrated from day one.
- {% highlight %}Tracify reaches production-ready evaluation in days; Langfuse reaches it in weeks.{% /highlight %}
- **Mitigation:** Time-to-value is Tracify's strategic advantage. Use it.

**2. Cost transparency is a business requirement.**
- Product leadership needs per-request costs, cost trending, and optimization recommendations. Finance needs visibility into LLM spend.
- You use multiple models (Claude, GPT-4, Mixtral) and need to understand the cost implications of routing decisions.
- **Mitigation:** Tracify's automatic cost calculation and per-call breakdown are hard to replicate in Langfuse without significant engineering.

**3. Your team is small (< 8 engineers).**
- Operational overhead of self-hosting (0.5-1.5 FTE) is a drag on product velocity.
- You cannot afford to dedicate engineering time to infrastructure operations.
- **Mitigation:** SaaS simplicity is worth the trade-off of data residency.

**4. Multi-turn agent interactions are central to your product.**
- Your agents handle conversation threads, maintain context across multiple steps, and users interact with the agent over time.
- Session-level cost and quality metrics are more useful than individual LLM call metrics.
- **Mitigation:** Tracify's native session tracking is simpler than building it yourself in Langfuse.

### Hybrid approach: Langfuse + external evaluation

Some teams use Langfuse for tracing and Tracify, Braintrust, or internal tools for evaluation. This approach:
- **Upside:** Best-of-breed for each component; custom evaluation logic supported.
- **Downside:** Maintains two integrations, cost tracking is manual, no native session management.
- **When to use:** Large teams with specialized evaluation requirements who can afford the operational overhead of two platforms.

> **Do not use both platforms "just in case."** Running both platforms adds cost and confusion. Make a decision based on your primary constraints. You can migrate later if your constraints change.

## Decision framework for practitioners and leaders

### For technical practitioners

Use this checklist to decide:

| Question | Score if yes | Suggests |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Must production data stay in your VPC? | +10 Langfuse | Data residency is non-negotiable |
| Is evaluation velocity your primary blocker? | +5 Tracify | You need to iterate on quality gates fast |
| Do you have >1.5 FTE available for platform operations? | +4 Langfuse | Self-hosting is operationally viable |
| Are you using Langfuse in production already? | +3 Langfuse | Migration costs are significant |
| Do you need LLM-as-judge evaluation built-in? | +5 Tracify | Langfuse requires external tooling |
| Is cost transparency a Q1 business requirement? | +5 Tracify | Automatic cost calculation is non-negotiable |
| Do you prefer to audit and modify source code? | +3 Langfuse | Open-source gives you control |

**Scoring:** If Langfuse score > 15, self-hosting is worth considering. If Tracify score > 10, SaaS speed is your advantage. If both are high, revisit your constraints; you may need a hybrid approach.

### For engineering/product leaders

**Time-to-production:** Do you have two weeks or two months?
- **Two weeks:** Tracify. Langfuse requires setup time you don't have.
- **Two months:** Either. Langfuse becomes viable if evaluation complexity justifies the setup cost.

**Team size:** How many engineers can we dedicate to observability?
- **< 5 engineers:** Tracify. Ops overhead kills productivity.
- **5-15 engineers:** Either. Langfuse is viable if infrastructure skills are present.
- **> 15 engineers:** Either. Infrastructure expertise scales; Langfuse becomes operationally realistic.

**Compliance posture:** Do we need to keep data in our VPC?
- **Yes:** Langfuse self-hosted only. This is non-negotiable.
- **No:** Either. Tracify's SaaS model eliminates operational overhead.

**Evaluation strategy:** What does production confidence look like?
- **"We need to catch regressions fast":** Tracify. Built-in evaluation is a force multiplier.
- **"We have custom ML evaluation requirements":** Langfuse. You own the evaluation pipeline.

## Migration paths and reversibility

### From Langfuse to Tracify (low-risk)

If you already have Langfuse instrumentation and want to evaluate Tracify:

1. **Parallel running (weeks 1-4):**
   - Add Tracify SDK to your codebase alongside Langfuse.
   - Instrument once; send identical spans to both platforms.
   - Cost: minimal (both receive the same span data).

2. **Evaluation comparison (weeks 2-4):**
   - Create equivalent test cases in Tracify.
   - Run evaluations on Tracify; compare results to Langfuse evaluations.
   - Verify that Tracify's cost calculations align with your expectations.

3. **Decision point (week 4):**
   - If Tracify evaluation quality is acceptable, migrate fully.
   - If you prefer Langfuse, remove Tracify instrumentation and continue.

4. **Migration (if proceeding):**
   - Redirect instrumentation to Tracify.
   - Keep Langfuse running for 2 more weeks as a safety net.
   - Shut down Langfuse once Tracify is stable.

**Total migration risk:** Low. You can rollback to Langfuse within 24 hours.

### From Tracify to Langfuse (costly)

If you need self-hosting after using Tracify:

1. **Export your data (week 1):**
   - Use Tracify's API to export traces and evaluation results.
   - Prepare Langfuse self-hosted environment.

2. **Replay and rebuild (weeks 2-4):**
   - Replay traces into Langfuse self-hosted.
   - Recreate test cases and evaluation logic.
   - This is manual work; no automated migration tool exists.

3. **Parallel validation (weeks 4-6):**
   - Run both systems for 2 weeks.
   - Verify trace fidelity, cost calculations, and evaluation results.

4. **Cutover (week 6+):**
   - Migrate all instrumentation to Langfuse.
   - Decommission Tracify.

**Total migration risk:** Moderate-to-high. Rebuilding evaluation logic takes 2-4 weeks. Avoid this scenario by making the self-hosting decision early.

## Action checklist for decision-makers

- [ ] **Compliance check:** Document data residency requirements. If VPC-only is required, Langfuse self-hosted is your path.
- [ ] **Scope check:** Estimate monthly LLM call volume, team size, and time-to-production deadline.
- [ ] **Cost check:** Calculate real TCO for both platforms: platform costs + engineering time (0.5-2 FTE for Langfuse).
- [ ] **Evaluation check:** Identify your quality gates. Verify that the platform's evaluation model supports them.
- [ ] **Operations check:** Assess your team's appetite for infrastructure operations (self-hosted) vs. operational simplicity (SaaS).
- [ ] **Prototype:** If undecided, run a 1-week parallel pilot with both platforms. SDKs are compatible; you can run both simultaneously.
- [ ] **Reversibility:** Plan your migration path and decision point. Make it easy to switch in the first month.
- [ ] **Review:** Set a 30-day review date to assess traces, cost accuracy, and evaluation framework fit.

If you are optimizing for speed and integrated evaluation, start with [Tracify's agent observability guide](/blog/ai-agent-observability-complete-guide) to understand the full platform. If you are prioritizing self-hosting and architectural control, Langfuse's [self-hosting documentation](https://langfuse.com/docs/deployment/self-host) and community forums are excellent resources.

## Frequently asked questions

{% faq-item question="Is Langfuse expensive to self-host?" answer="Not inherently, but the full cost is higher than you might initially think. Break it down: infrastructure (Postgres + Redis on Kubernetes: $1,200-2,000/month), initial setup (2-3 weeks), and ongoing maintenance (0.5-1.5 FTE for monitoring, scaling, and patching). The total annual cost is $70k-120k depending on your scale and team expertise. This is often lower than SaaS platform costs over time, but it trades fixed infrastructure expenses for variable engineering time. For small teams (< 8 engineers), this is rarely worth it. For large teams with mature infrastructure practices, it becomes viable." /%}

{% faq-item question="Can I run Langfuse and Tracify together during evaluation?" answer="Yes, absolutely. Both platforms can ingest the same span data. Instrument your code once, send traces to both platforms simultaneously, and compare evaluation results side-by-side. This is a low-risk way to validate Tracify's evaluation quality before committing fully. Run parallel for 2-4 weeks; the cost is minimal (both ingest the same data). This approach lets you make a reversible decision. If Tracify evaluation quality is acceptable, migrate fully. If you prefer Langfuse, remove Tracify instrumentation." /%}

{% faq-item question="Does Langfuse support cost tracking like Tracify?" answer="Langfuse provides token counting at the span level (model, input tokens, output tokens), but it does not know the cost of those tokens. To get per-call cost breakdown like Tracify, you must: (1) integrate provider pricing data manually (fetch from OpenAI, Anthropic, etc.), (2) calculate cost in your data warehouse or post-processing scripts, or (3) use a third-party cost tracking tool. This is doable but requires engineering work and ongoing maintenance as provider pricing changes. Tracify handles this automatically by fetching real-time pricing from providers." /%}

{% faq-item question="How difficult is evaluation on Langfuse compared to Tracify?" answer="On Langfuse, evaluation is manual and requires external tooling. You get the trace data; you provide the evaluation logic. This means: (1) manually reviewing traces and marking them good/bad, (2) building external evaluation runners that call the Langfuse API, pulling traces, and running your test cases, and (3) managing test case versions, results storage, and regression detection yourself. This is appropriate if your evaluation is highly specialized or uses proprietary ML models. On Tracify, evaluation is built-in: create test cases in the dashboard, run batch evaluations with a click, and get regression alerts automatically. The trade-off is simplicity vs. customization." /%}

{% faq-item question="When would I genuinely prefer Langfuse over Tracify?" answer="Choose Langfuse when: (1) data residency in your VPC is non-negotiable (regulated industries—Langfuse self-hosting is the only option), (2) your evaluation requirements are highly specialized and don't fit a standard test case model, (3) you have infrastructure expertise and value architectural control, or (4) you already have Langfuse instrumentation and the migration cost is high. For most teams optimizing for production velocity, cost visibility, and evaluation iteration speed, Tracify's all-in-one model delivers faster time-to-insight and lower total cost of ownership. The decision usually boils down to: Can you self-host? (Yes → Langfuse is viable). Do you need integrated evaluation? (Yes → Tracify is faster). Do you have time for platform operations? (No → Tracify wins)." /%}
